# jotpost behind an existing Apache TLS vhost.
#
# WHY THIS RATHER THAN OPENING PORT 7701:
#   - No new open port. A port scan of the house already finds 443 open and expects to; it learns
#     nothing new. Quieting jotpost's own responses cannot achieve this - nmap decides a port is
#     open from the TCP handshake, before any HTTP byte is exchanged, so a forwarded 7701 reports
#     `open` no matter what the service answers.
#   - TLS for free. jotpost speaks plain HTTP. Forwarded directly, the bearer token and the full
#     text of every jot cross the internet in cleartext.
#   - One thing to keep patched and rate-limited, and it is the one already built for the job.
#
# Goes inside the *:443 vhost for www.azvenigo.com, on whichever host terminates TLS - NOT on
# azzorin, which only runs jotpost itself. Needs: a2enmod proxy proxy_http rewrite
#
#   192.168.1.112:7701 = azzorin, per packaging/systemd/jotpost.service.

    # Non-POST gets Apache's own 404, rendered by Apache, before the backend is ever contacted -
    # so `GET /jot` is byte-identical to `GET /any-dead-path`, same status, same body, same length.
    # <LimitExcept POST> would answer 403, which is itself the tell we are trying not to give.
    RewriteEngine On
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_METHOD} !=POST
    RewriteRule ^/jot$ - [R=404,L]

    <Location /jot>
        ProxyPass         http://192.168.1.112:7701/jot connectiontimeout=5 timeout=15
        ProxyPassReverse  http://192.168.1.112:7701/jot

        # The other half of looking like nothing. jotpost answers every rejection with the same
        # 15-byte "404 Not Found"; without this, that body is distinguishable from the site's real
        # 404 page by Content-Length alone (15 vs 541 as of this writing), which is enough to mark
        # /jot as special. ProxyErrorOverride swaps any backend 4xx/5xx body for this
        # site's own ErrorDocument, keeping the status code. 2xx is passed through untouched, so a
        # successful 204 still reaches the client as a 204.
        ProxyErrorOverride On

        # Matches kMaxBodyBytes in jotpost/main.cpp. Rejected here, so an oversized body never
        # crosses the LAN.
        LimitRequestBody 16384
    </Location>

# HOW TO VERIFY THIS - and how NOT to
#
# The ONLY valid test is a POST carrying a well-formed body, which must return 204:
#
#   curl -i -X POST https://www.azvenigo.com/jot -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#        --data-binary '{"ts":"2026-01-01T00:00:00","entry":"proxy test"}'
#
# and then confirm the line actually landed in /mnt/fast/web/jot_log/jots.json on azzorin. Delete
# it afterwards - Loom's WatchList ingests that file, and a test line becomes a real jot within
# minutes.
#
# Every cheaper check is worthless here, because it exercises a request jotpost would reject
# anyway: a GET is 404'd by the RewriteRule before it is ever proxied, and a POST with an empty or
# malformed body fails jotpost's own validation. All of them return the same 404 whether the proxy
# works or was never installed.
#
# That is not an accident, it is ProxyErrorOverride doing its job - and it means THIS ENDPOINT
# CANNOT BE DIAGNOSED FROM THE PUBLIC URL. A broken proxy and an unconfigured path are byte
# identical: same status, same length, same ETag. Comparing /jot against a dead path proves
# nothing. It has already produced one confident wrong diagnosis. When something is off, read the
# vhost access log on the Apache host and jots.json on azzorin; the status code will not tell you
# where the chain broke.
#
# ORDERING GOTCHA, the first thing to check if a valid POST 404s: mod_rewrite runs in the
# URL-translation phase, BEFORE <Location> sections are resolved. An existing RewriteRule earlier
# in the vhost that rewrites or claims the URI will stop this ProxyPass from ever being consulted,
# and Apache falls through to the filesystem and serves an ordinary 404. Placing this block near
# an existing root-block RewriteRule is exactly where that bites.
#
# STILL TO DO ON THE JOTPOST SIDE, before anything off-LAN can route here:
#
#   1. Set a token. Without one jotpost accepts from anyone who can reach it, and after this change
#      "anyone who can reach it" includes the internet:
#
#        umask 077; head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64 > /etc/loom/jotpost.token
#        chown alex:alex /etc/loom/jotpost.token
#
#      then add --token-file=/etc/loom/jotpost.token to ExecStart. Use --token-file rather than
#      --token: jotpost.service is public in this repo, and --token=SECRET is visible in `ps` to
#      every user on the box.
#
#   2. Firewall 7701 to the Apache host only, so the proxy is the sole path in and the LAN-side
#      port cannot be reached from anywhere a guest VLAN or a compromised device can route:
#
#        ufw allow proto tcp from <apache-host-ip> to 192.168.1.112 port 7701
#
#   3. Consider a path nobody would guess - /jot is a word a wordlist scanner will try. Change the
#      RewriteRule, the <Location> and the first ProxyPass argument together (the trailing /jot on
#      ProxyPass is the backend's route name and must stay /jot, since that is what jotpost serves):
#
#        RewriteRule ^/k7f3q9x2$ ...   <Location /k7f3q9x2>   ProxyPass http://192.168.1.112:7701/jot
#
#      Obscurity, not security - the token is what actually protects this. It just keeps the
#      endpoint out of the logs of every scanner that walks a common-paths list.
#
#   4. Rate limiting is not configured here. Apache's mod_ratelimit shapes bandwidth, not request
#      rate; fail2ban on the access log, or mod_qos, is the real answer if this ever gets found.
